README.md-375-
README.md:376:> Note: On 2023-07-27, AWS Health released the Delegated Admin feature which enables AHA deployments in member accounts without the extra steps below.
README.md:377:See: [Using AWS Health Delegated Administrator with AHA](#using-aws-health-delegated-administrator-with-aha)
README.md-378-
README.md:379:1. [Enable Health Organizational View](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/health/latest/ug/enable-organizational-view.html) from the console or CLI, so that you can aggregate Health events for all accounts in your AWS Organization. 
README.md-380-2. Have at least 1 [endpoint](#configuring-an-endpoint) configured (you can have multiple)
README.md-381-
README.md:382:**NOTE: ** For Multi region deployment, DynamoDB table will be created with PAY_PER_REQUEST billing mode insted of PROVISIONED due to limitation with terraform.
README.md-383-
README.md:384:### Deployment - Terraform
README.md-385-
README.md-386-1. Clone the AHA package that from this repository. If you're not familiar with the process, [here](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-clone) is some documentation. The URL to clone is in the upper right-hand corner labeled `Clone uri`
README.md-387-```
README.md:388:$ git clone https://github.com/aws-samples/aws-health-aware.git
README.md-389-```
README.md:390:2. In your top-level management account deploy terraform module Terraform_MGMT_ROLE.tf to create Cross-Account Role for PHD access
README.md-391-```
README.md:392:$ cd aws-health-aware/terraform/Terraform_MGMT_ROLE
README.md:393:$ terraform init
README.md:394:$ terraform plan
README.md:395:$ terraform apply
README.md-396- Input *OrgMemberAccountId*  Enter the account id of the member account you plan to run AHA in (e.g. 000123456789).
README.md-397-```
README.md:398:3. Wait for deployment to complete. This will create an IAM role with the necessary AWS Organizations and AWS Health API permissions for the member account to assume. and note the **AWSHealthAwareRoleForPHDEventsArn** role name, this will be used during deploying solution in member account
README.md:399:4. In the *Outputs* section, there will be a value for *AWSHealthAwareRoleForPHDEventsArn* (e.g. arn:aws:iam::000123456789:role/aha-org-role-AWSHealthAwareRoleForPHDEvents-ABCSDE12201), copy that down as you will need to update params file (variable ManagementAccountRoleArn).
README.md:400:4. Change directory to **terraform/Terraform_DEPLOY_AHA** to deploy the solution
README.md:401:5. Update parameters file **terraform.tfvars** as below
README.md-402- - *aha_primary_region* - change to region where you want to deploy AHA solution
README.md-403- - *aha_secondary_region* - Required if needed to deploy in AHA solution in multiple regions, change to another region (Secondary) where you want to deploy AHA solution, Otherwise leave to default empty value.
README.md:404: - *AWSOrganizationsEnabled* - change the value to `Yes`. If you do NOT have AWS Organizations enabled you should be following the steps for [AHA for users who are NOT using AWS Organizations](#aha-without-aws-organizations-using-terraform)
README.md:405: - *AWSHealthEventType* - select whether you want to receive *all* event types or *only* issues.
README.md-406- - *Communications Channels* section - enter the URLs, Emails and/or ARN of the endpoints you configured previously.
README.md-407- - *Email Setup* section - enter the From and To Email addresses as well as the Email subject. If you aren't configuring email, just leave it as is.
README.md-408- - *EventSearchBack* - enter in the amount of hours you want to search back for events. Default is 1 hour.
README.md-409- - *Regions* enter in the regions you want to search for events in. Default is all regions. You can filter for up to 10, comma separated (e.g. us-east-1, us-east-2).
README.md:410: - *ManagementAccountRoleArn* -  Enter in the full IAM arn from step 10 (e.g. arn:aws:iam::000123456789:role/aha-org-role-AWSHealthAwareRoleForPHDEvents-ABCSDE12201)
README.md-411- - *S3Bucket* - type ***just*** the name of the S3 bucket where exclude file .csv you upload. leave it empty if exclude Account feature is not used. 
README.md-412- - *ExcludeAccountIDs* - type ***just*** the name of the .csv file you want to upload if needed to exclude accounts from monitoring, else leave it to empty.
README.md:413:4. Deploy the solution using terraform commands below.
README.md-414-```
README.md:415:$ terraform init
README.md:416:$ terraform plan
README.md:417:$ terraform apply
README.md-418-```
README.md-419-
--
README.md-429-**If for some reason, you still have issues after updating, you can easily just delete the stack and redeploy. The infrastructure can be destroyed and rebuilt within minutes through CloudFormation.**
README.md-430-
README.md:431:# Updating using Terraform
README.md-432-**Until this project is migrated to the AWS Serverless Application Model (SAM), updates will have to be done as described below:**
README.md-433-1. Pull the latest code from git repository for AHA.
README.md:434:2. Update the parameters file terraform.tfvars per your requirement
README.md:435:3. Copy the terraform template files to directory where your previous state exists
README.md-436-4. Deploy the templates as below
README.md-437-```
README.md:438:$ cd aws-health-aware
README.md:439:$ git pull https://github.com/aws-samples/aws-health-aware.git
README.md:440:$ cd terraform/Terraform_DEPLOY_AHA
README.md:441:$ terraform init
README.md:442:$ terraform plan - This command should show any difference existing config and latest code.
README.md:443:$ terraform apply
README.md-444-```
README.md-445-
README.md:446:**If for some reason, you still have issues after updating, you can easily just delete the stack and redeploy. The infrastructure can be destroyed and rebuilt within minutes through Terraform.**
